  "worked_solution": "We note that $p=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nThe equation has the denominators $p$ and $9p.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(p,9p)=9p.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $9p,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{p} & =\\frac{10}{9p}+\\frac{p-1}{9p} \\\\ 9p\\cdot (\\frac{1}{p}) & =9p\\cdot (\\frac{10}{9p}+\\frac{p-1}{9p}) \\\\ 9p\\cdot \\frac{1}{p} & =9p\\cdot \\frac{10}{9p}+9p\\cdot \\frac{p-1}{9p} \\\\ 9\\cancel{p}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{p}} & =\\cancel{9p}\\cdot \\frac{10}{\\cancel{9p}}+\\cancel{9p}\\cdot \\frac{p-1}{\\cancel{9p}} \\\\ 9\\cdot (1) & =10+p-1 \\\\ 9 & =10+p-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $p,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =10+p-1 \\\\ 9 & =p+9 \\\\ p & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $p=0$ as a solution. Since $p=0,$ we conclude that there is no solution.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
